  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "After many years of work, Chef has managed to open an art gallery showcasing all his pieces.\n\nThere are NNN pieces of art, arranged in a line from left to right.\n\nThe iii-th piece from the left has a beauty of AiA_iAi​.\n\nEach AiA_iAi​ equals either 111 or 222.\n\nA famous art critic is visiting the gallery, and Chef knows that he will move through the gallery as follows.\n\nInitially, the critic will start off by viewing the first art piece. Then, if ppp denotes the index of the piece currently being viewed,\n\n\nIf Ap=1A_p = 1Ap​=1, the critic will move to piece p+1p+1p+1.\nIf Ap=2A_p = 2Ap​=2, the critic will be absorbed in the beauty of what he just saw, and so will move to piece p+2p+2p+2 without viewing piece p+1p+1p+1.\nMore generally, starting from p=1p = 1p=1, after viewing piece ppp the critic will move to piece p+App + A_pp+Ap​.\n\nIf p+Ap&gt;Np + A_p \\gt Np+Ap​&gt;N, the critic will not view any more pieces.\n\nFor an arrangement of art pieces, let f(A)f(A)f(A) denote the number of them that the critic will view.\n\nChef wants f(A)f(A)f(A) to be as **large** as possible.\n\nTo achieve this, he can swap adjacent art pieces.\n\nFunctionally, this corresponds to choosing an index iii (1≤i&lt;N1 \\le i \\lt N1≤i&lt;N) and performing swap(Ai,Ai+1)\\text{swap}(A_i, A_{i+1})swap(Ai​,Ai+1​).\n\nYour task is to find the **minimum number** of such adjacent swaps needed to attain the maximum possible value of f(A)f(A)f(A).\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: the minimum number of adjacent swaps needed to maximize f(A)f(A)f(A).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ai≤21 \\le A_i \\le 21≤Ai​≤2",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n3\n2 1 2\n4\n2 2 2 2\n5\n1 2 1 2 1\n10\n2 1 1 2 1 2 1 2 2 1",
        "output": "0\n0\n1\n4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: With the given arrangement, the critic will behave as follows:\n\nStart at p=1p = 1p=1 and view the first piece.\nThen, because Ap=2A_p = 2Ap​=2, move to p+2=3p+2 = 3p+2=3.\nView the piece at p=3p = 3p=3.\nThen, because Ap=2A_p = 2Ap​=2 and p+2>Np+2 \\gt Np+2>N, no more pieces will be viewed.\n\nSo, f(A)=2f(A) = 2f(A)=2 for the initial arrangement.\nIt can be shown that no matter what Chef does, it's impossible to make the critic view all 333 pieces. So, the maximum possible f(A)f(A)f(A) equals 222, and no swaps are needed.\nTest case 222: All values of AiA_iAi​ are the same, so swapping cannot change anything.\nTest case 333: The initial arrangement has f(A)=3f(A) = 3f(A)=3, because the critic will view pieces 1,2,1, 2,1,2, and 444.\nHowever, if Chef swaps the last two pieces to obtain the arrangement [1,2,1,1,2][1, 2, 1, 1, 2][1,2,1,1,2], the critic will view pieces 1,2,4,51, 2, 4, 51,2,4,5 which is better.\nIt's impossible for all 555 pieces to be viewed, so 444 is the best and one swap is needed to get there."
      }
    ]
